<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferentino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ferentinum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferentino, ancient Ferentinum, is a hill town of the Hernici in the Sacco valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It is known for its massive polygonal walls and its acropolis of the pre-Roman and Roman periods. It stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the Hernican revolt of 306 BCE and kept its independence as an ally before becoming a Roman municipium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferentino.jpg" alt="Ferentino" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fiat 500e · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiesa_di_Santa_Maria_Maggiore_(Ferentino).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ferentino is a town and comune in the province of Frosinone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, 65 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It lies on a hill 400 metres above sea level, in the Monti Ernici area. In antiquity it was the town of the Hernici of Ferentinum. It stood just above the Via Latina and, being a strong place, served for the detention of hostages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ferentinum took no part in the rising of 306 BCE. Its inhabitants became Roman citizens after 195 BCE, and the place later became a municipium. From 1198 to 1557 Ferentino was the seat of the papal rectorate of Campagna and Marittima province. The town was strong in textiles, linen and embroidery, and in handicraft such as clay bricks, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it saw heavy industrial growth, mainly in pharmaceuticals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town keeps remains of ancient fortifications. The lower part of the outer walls is built of roughly hewn blocks of limestone, with rectangular blocks of tuff above in places. Two gates survive, the Porta Maggiore, a double gate built wholly of tuff blocks, and the Porta Sanguinaria. The acropolis, dating from the 2nd to 1st centuries BCE, has massive retaining walls like those of the lower town, and a building of travertine blocks whose cornice carries two inscriptions of the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> recording its construction by two censors.</p><p>The Cathedral of Sts John and Paul stands on part of the ancient acropolis, rebuilt in 1099 to 1118 on the site of an older church. It contains a Cosmatesque ciborium and a 12th-century mosaic pavement. The Gothic church of Santa Maria Maggiore dates from the 13th and 14th centuries. The Romanesque monastery of Sant'Antonio Abate housed the remains of Pope Celestine V until 1327.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferentino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
